## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to design a privacy-conscious, scalable fraud-detection pipeline across multiple tenants with governance and cost controls.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation
- Privacy-preserving aggregation (secure sum, differential privacy)
- Vertex AI Pipelines and canary rollout
- Dataflow enrichment, BigQuery provenance
- Data Catalog policy tagging
- Drift detection and explainability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo example: privacy budget check
function shouldRollout(latencyMs, drift, budget) {
  const okLatency = latencyMs < 250;
  const okDrift = drift < 0.1;
  const okBudget = budget.remaining >= budget.required;
  return okLatency && okDrift && okBudget;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement per-tenant feature stores in Vertex AI Feature Store?
- How would you simulate a canary rollout with rollbacks if fraud latency spikes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design multi-tenant inference with strict data residency, budgets, and auditable lineage in a real-world GCP setup.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation in Vertex AI Endpoints and Feature Store namespaces
- Routing logic with per-tenant budget and latency SLAs
- Data Catalog metadata tagging for lineage and governance
- Drift detection and rollback triggers per tenant

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo routing function
async function routeRequest(tenantId, req) {
  const budgetOk = checkBudget(tenantId);
  const latencyOk = checkLatency(tenantId);
  const endpoint = budgetOk && latencyOk ? getTenantEndpoint(tenantId) : getSharedEndpoint();
  return await predict(endpoint, req);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you enforce per-tenant quotas in Feature Store and endpoints?
- How would you simulate rollback scenarios and validate audit trails?
